16:["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-3 pt-3 border-t border-amber-200 text-gray-900","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Your task: "}],"Learn to select an office visit level from what the documentation actually supports."]}]
17:["$","p","1",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Evaluation and Management codes describe cognitive work: the assessment, the decision making, the counseling. They are the highest-volume codes in outpatient medicine and the most scrutinized, because level selection has historically been inconsistent."}]
18:["$","div","2",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-blue-50 border-blue-400","children":[false,"Office visit E/M coding changed substantially in 2021. The old system counted history and examination bullets to arrive at a level. Those elements must still be medically appropriate and documented, but they no longer determine the level. Level is now selected by either medical decision making or total time on the date of the encounter — the coder's choice, whichever supports the higher level. Older training material still teaches the bullet-counting method; it does not apply to office visits."]}]
19:["$","figure","3",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200","children":["$","table",null,{"className":"w-full text-left border-collapse","children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50","children":[["$","th","0",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Patient type"}],["$","th","1",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Codes"}],["$","th","2",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Definition"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"New patient"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"99202-99205"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Has not received professional services from the provider, or another of the same specialty in the same group, within three years"}]]}],["$","tr","1",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Established patient"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"99211-99215"}],["$","td","2",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Has received such services within three years"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"Office visit code families"}]]}]
1a:["$","p","4",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"The three-year rule matters financially: new patient visits pay more, because more work is involved in an initial assessment. It turns on the same specialty and same group practice, not merely the same individual provider — a patient seen by a different physician in your practice's same specialty within three years is established."}]
1b:["$","figure","5",{"children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-gray-200","children":["$","table",null,{"className":"w-full text-left border-collapse","children":[["$","thead",null,{"children":["$","tr",null,{"className":"bg-gray-50","children":[["$","th","0",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Element"}],["$","th","1",{"scope":"col","className":"px-4 py-3 text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200","children":"Considers"}]]}]}],["$","tbody",null,{"children":[["$","tr","0",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Number and complexity of problems addressed"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"How many problems, and how serious or unstable"}]]}],["$","tr","1",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Amount and complexity of data reviewed"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Tests ordered or reviewed, records obtained, discussion with other providers"}]]}],["$","tr","2",{"className":"even:bg-gray-50/50","children":[["$","td","0",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Risk of complications or morbidity"}],["$","td","1",{"className":"px-4 py-3 text-gray-700 border-b border-gray-100 align-top leading-relaxed","children":"Risk from the problems, the diagnostic testing, and the treatment chosen"}]]}]]}]]}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"className":"text-sm text-gray-500 mt-2 italic","children":"The three elements of medical decision making"}]]}]
1c:["$","p","6",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Two of the three elements must be met or exceeded at a given level to support it. The levels run straightforward, low, moderate, and high, corresponding to codes 99202/99212 through 99205/99215."}]
1d:["$","div","7",{"className":"rounded-xl border border-primary-200 bg-primary-50/50 p-6","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"block text-xs font-semibold uppercase tracking-wide text-primary-700 mb-2","children":"Worked example"}],["$","h4",null,{"className":"text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-1","children":"Selecting a level two ways"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-5","children":"An established patient is seen for two chronic conditions, one uncontrolled. The provider reviews recent labs, adjusts a prescription, and documents 40 minutes total on the date of the encounter."}],["$","ol",null,{"className":"space-y-4","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":1}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Try medical decision making first."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Two chronic conditions with one uncontrolled generally supports moderate complexity on the problems element. Reviewing labs contributes to the data element. Prescription drug management is a recognized moderate-risk item. Two of three at moderate supports 99214."}]]}]]}],["$","li","1",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":2}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Then check total time as an alternative."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"Time is the other permitted route, and you may use whichever supports the higher level. Total time includes the provider's non-face-to-face work on that date — reviewing records beforehand, documenting afterwards — not only the time in the room."}]]}]]}],["$","li","2",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":3}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Compare 40 minutes against the time thresholds for established patient visits."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"40 minutes falls in the range for 99215 rather than 99214. Here time supports a higher level than medical decision making does, so time is the better basis — provided the total time is actually documented."}]]}]]}],["$","li","3",{"className":"flex gap-4","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"flex-shrink-0 w-7 h-7 rounded-full bg-primary-600 text-white flex items-center justify-center text-sm font-bold","children":4}],["$","div",null,{"className":"flex-1","children":[["$","p",null,{"className":"font-semibold text-gray-900","children":"Confirm the documentation states total time explicitly."}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-1","children":"This is where time-based selection fails. A note saying '40 minutes spent, including review of labs and counseling on medication changes' supports it. A note with no time statement cannot support time-based selection at all, no matter how long the visit actually took."}]]}]]}]]}],["$","p",null,{"className":"mt-5 pt-4 border-t border-primary-200 text-gray-800","children":[["$","span",null,{"className":"font-semibold","children":"Result: "}],"99215, supported by documented total time, with medical decision making independently supporting at least 99214."]}],"$L23"]}]
1e:["$","div","8",{"className":"p-4 rounded-xl border-l-4 text-gray-800 leading-relaxed bg-yellow-50 border-yellow-400","children":[false,"A consistent pattern of coding the same level for every visit — whether that is always 99213 or always 99214 — attracts payer attention regardless of which level it is. Real patient panels produce a distribution across levels. Coding defensively low is not a safe strategy; it is a different pattern anomaly, and it costs the practice revenue it legitimately earned."]}]
1f:["$","$L24","9",{"block":{"type":"knowledge-check","question":"A patient last seen by a different physician of the same specialty in your practice two years ago returns. Which code family applies?","options":["New patient, because this is a different physician","Established patient, because the same specialty in the same group saw them within three years","New patient, because more than one year has passed","Either may be used, at the coder's discretion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The rule turns on the same specialty within the same group practice, not the individual provider. Two years is inside the three-year window, so this is an established patient — and coding it as new would be upcoding, since new patient visits pay more.","optionRationales":["The rule covers the group and specialty, not just the individual physician.","Correct. Same specialty, same group, within three years means established.","The window is three years, not one.","There is no discretion. The three-year rule determines it."]}}]
20:["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-12","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"mb-8","children":[["$","h2",null,{"className":"text-3xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3","children":"Challenge"}],["$","p",null,{"className":"text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed max-w-3xl","children":"Apply what you've learned in this lesson."}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"bg-white rounded-2xl shadow-md border border-gray-200 p-8","children":["$","div",null,{"className":"space-y-6","children":[["$","p","0",{"className":"text-gray-700 leading-relaxed","children":"Practice level selection against real documentation standards. Use the current CPT E/M guidelines."}],["$","ol","1",{"start":"$undefined","className":"list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3 text-gray-700 marker:font-semibold marker:text-primary-600","children":[["$","li","0",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Read the E/M guidelines at the front of the CPT Evaluation and Management section. Write down the time thresholds for 99212 through 99215."}],["$","li","1",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"For an established patient with one stable chronic condition, no tests ordered, and no prescription change, determine the supportable level using medical decision making and justify it."}],["$","li","2",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"For the same encounter documented as 30 minutes total time, determine the level using time. Explain which basis you would use and why."}],["$","li","3",{"className":"leading-relaxed pl-1","children":"Write three sentences a provider could add to a note that would support time-based selection, and explain what makes them sufficient."}]]}]]}]}]]}]
